ForkJoinPool y work-stealing:

**Diseño**:
- N workers (default = cores).
- Cada worker tiene un deque (double-ended queue).
- Push nuevas tasks al TOP de su own deque.
- Pop del TOP para trabajar (LIFO — cache-friendly, recentmost).
- Cuando su deque está vacío, roba del BOTTOM de OTHER worker deque (FIFO — take viejos primero).

**Por qué work-stealing**:
- Sin central queue → no contention en la queue.
- Locality: tu propio deque tiene tus recientes → cache warm.
- Robo del OTHER worker: menos frequency, menos contention.

**Ejemplo**:
```java
class SumTask extends RecursiveTask<Long> {
    long[] arr; int lo, hi;

    protected Long compute() {
        if (hi - lo < THRESHOLD) {
            long sum = 0;
            for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++) sum += arr[i];
            return sum;
        }
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        SumTask left = new SumTask(arr, lo, mid);
        SumTask right = new SumTask(arr, mid, hi);
        left.fork();  // async execute
        long r = right.compute();  // this thread computes
        long l = left.join();  // wait for left
        return l + r;
    }
}
```

**Sweet spots**:
- Recursive divide-and-conquer.
- Balanced work (unbalanced trees can perform poorly).
- CPU-bound work (I/O blocks workers).

**Anti-patterns**:
- Fork/Join for I/O-bound: workers blocked, pool underutilized.
- Very small tasks: overhead > benefit.
- Blocking operations inside compute() (usa ManagedBlocker si necesario).

**parallelStream() usa ForkJoinPool.commonPool()**. Si tienes I/O en un parallelStream, contamina el commonPool — usa custom pool o CompletableFuture.
